# Local-dev defaults for the Go backend. `godotenv` loads `.env` on startup
# (see internal/config/config.go), so copy this to `.env` and adjust if your
# local Postgres isn't on the default port/credentials. See
# apps/backend/scripts/setup-dev-db.sql for the matching bootstrap.

POSTGRES_URL=postgres://kaata:kaata_dev@localhost:5432/kaata?sslmode=disable
BACKEND_PORT=8080

# Operator analytics dashboard secret. Guards GET /v1/admin/stats (the /admin web
# page). When UNSET, the admin routes 404 (dashboard disabled) — safe default.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32. The web build's VITE_ADMIN_API_KEY (or the
# paste-once login on /admin) must match this value.
ADMIN_API_KEY=

# Analytics noise filter for the admin dashboard. Your OWN signed-in account
# UUID(s) and home/office IP(s), comma-separated — excluded from every funnel
# number so your test devices and reinstalls don't inflate the stats. Applied
# at QUERY time, so it's retroactive (fixes already-recorded rows the moment you
# set it) and reversible (clear it → rows reappear). account_id is the robust
# key: it survives reinstalls once you've signed in on a test device.
# Find yours: docker exec ... psql -c "SELECT id FROM accounts WHERE email='you@gmail.com';"
# OPERATOR_ACCOUNT_IDS=uuid1,uuid2
# OPERATOR_IPS=1.2.3.4,5.6.7.8

# Target of the /v1/download redirect. In local dev we point at the Vite
# dev server which serves apps/web/public/downloads/ at root. The web
# button hits the backend's /v1/download which 302s to this URL.
APK_DOWNLOAD_URL=http://localhost:3000/downloads/kaata-0.1.0.apk

# Soft-migration signal. When set, every check-in response carries this URL
# in the `migrate_to_backend_url` field. Mobile clients persist it locally
# and switch to it on their next launch. Use this to move the backend to a
# new domain without forcing users to reinstall.
#   - Leave empty (or unset): no migration signal — clients stay on the
#     URL they were built with (EXPO_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL).
#   - Set to "https://new.example.com": clients migrate to that URL.
#   - Set to "" (empty string) explicitly: clears any prior override on
#     clients, reverting them to their build-time default.
# MIGRATE_TO_BACKEND_URL=

# Canonical public SITE origin. Used to build shared-ledger links
# (kaata.af/v/<token>) and their OG preview `og:url`. Defaults to
# https://kaata.af; override only for a staging domain.
# WEB_BASE_URL=https://kaata.af

# Origin used to BUILD the WhatsApp share link + its og:url. Defaults to
# WEB_BASE_URL (kaata.af). Per-customer WhatsApp previews need the share link to
# resolve to the backend's SSR page (GET /v/<token>) — the backend already
# serves that on its own domain, so setting this to the backend origin makes the
# preview work WITHOUT routing kaata.af/v/* to the backend. The page's chrome
# links still point at the canonical site (WEB_BASE_URL).
# SHARE_LINK_BASE_URL=https://api.kaata.af

# The backend's OWN public origin, baked into the shared-ledger SSR page so its
# inline script fetches the snapshot from an absolute URL. Set this in
# production (e.g. https://api.kaata.af) so that kaata.af/v/<token> is the ONLY
# path you need to route to the backend — the SSR page then talks to the API
# directly (CORS-enabled) instead of also needing kaata.af/v1/* proxied here.
# Leave empty for local dev (SSR + API are same-origin) or if /v1/* is proxied.
# PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://api.kaata.af

# Google OAuth web client ID. This is the AUDIENCE we validate against
# when verifying mobile-supplied Google ID tokens at /v1/auth/google.
# Get it from https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials — the
# "Web application" OAuth client (NOT the Android one). Public value;
# not a secret. The corresponding client *secret* is unused by us.
GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# HMAC secret for signing the session JWT we issue to mobile clients after
# a successful Google sign-in. Generate via `openssl rand -hex 32`. MUST
# be a long random string — anything short makes brute force feasible.
# Rotating invalidates every currently-issued session.
SESSION_JWT_SECRET=

# Phase 2 canonical name for the session JWT secret. Read first; falls back
# to SESSION_JWT_SECRET above for compatibility with v0.4 deployments.
# Same constraints — long random string, rotating invalidates every session.
JWT_SECRET=

# Phase 3 sync. Snapshot cron uses a separate Postgres pool so a slow
# snapshot replay (potentially scanning tens of thousands of events) can't
# starve user-facing request connections. Default is 4 connections; raise
# to 8 on bigger boxes. Capped at 64 to guard against typo-DOS.
# SNAPSHOT_DB_POOL_MAX=4

# Phase 4.1: stable UUID identifying the backend as an HLC author. Used
# as the device_id / hlc_device_id stamp on server-emitted
# vault_member_* events (AcceptInvite / ChangeRole / Revoke / Leave /
# TransferOwnership). If unset, defaults to the all-zero UUID, which is
# fine for single-replica deployments. For multi-replica setups,
# generate one UUID per replica so audit queries can attribute events
# to the emitting instance.
#
# SEC M1: REQUIRED when KAATA_ENV=production. The boot loop refuses to
# start without a parseable UUID in that mode. Generate with `uuidgen`
# and pin one per replica in your orchestrator's per-instance config.
# BACKEND_DEVICE_ID=

# KAATA_ENV: gates the production-only strictness checks (currently:
# BACKEND_DEVICE_ID required). Leave unset for dev / staging.
# KAATA_ENV=production

# Phase 4.1: archive-purge cron batch size. Per tick we scan at most
# this many vaults whose archived_at is older than 30 days. Capped at
# 1000 by the code. Tune up if backlog accumulates faster than the
# default 50/tick can clear it.
# ARCHIVE_PURGE_BATCH_SIZE=50

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 5 mesh (peer-to-peer vault sync via WebRTC + mDNS).
#
# The backend signs Vault Membership Credentials (VMCs) — short-lived
# (60-day) Ed25519-signed blobs that two phones exchange during mesh
# handshake to prove vault membership offline. This signing keypair is
# purely a mesh role; it is NOT the JWT secret and NOT the Google OAuth
# client.
#
# Generate a fresh keypair with:
#
#     cd apps/backend && go run ./cmd/genkeypair > .env.mesh
#
# Then commit:
#   - MESH_SIGNING_PUBKEY_PRIMARY to the mobile build's env (the client pins
#     it on first check-in and uses it to verify every VMC).
#   - MESH_SIGNING_PRIVKEY_PRIMARY to the backend's secret store ONLY.
#
# If MESH_SIGNING_PRIVKEY_PRIMARY is unset: the backend logs a critical
# warning at boot and refuses to issue VMCs (503 on /v1/vaults/:id/credential).
# Server sync continues to work normally; only peer-to-peer mesh is
# disabled, which is a graceful degradation rather than a hard failure.
MESH_SIGNING_PRIVKEY_PRIMARY=
MESH_SIGNING_PUBKEY_PRIMARY=

# Optional rotation pubkey. Operators set this during a key-rotation window
# so newly-shipped mobile clients pre-trust the upcoming pubkey. After the
# window completes (>= 60 days, every in-flight VMC has expired):
#   1. Promote: copy ROTATION values into PRIMARY, clear ROTATION.
#   2. Ship a mobile update that drops trust of the old pubkey.
# MESH_SIGNING_PUBKEY_ROTATION=
